[Line "5...exd4 6.Bc4 Nf6 7.O-O Bc5 8.Ng5 O-O 9.Nxf7: the pinned knight"]
[Section "5...exd4: the gambit"]
[Summary "Black grabs d4, White develops instead of recapturing. If Black castles into Ng5 and Nxf7, the knight on f6 is pinned along the f-file and White wins material."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 5. Nf3 exd4 { [%cal Rd4c3] Black takes the pawn (about one game in six).
The pawn on d4 is not going anywhere, so White does not recapture at once. } 6. Bc4 { [%cal Gc4f7,Gf3g5,Gf1f7,Gf3d4]
**Idea:** the Fantasy gambit. White ignores the pawn and develops with tempo: bishop to c4, castle, the
rook on the f-file, a knight to g5, so that f7 is attacked three times before Black has castled. The pawn
on d4 can be taken back with Nxd4 or Qxd4 whenever it suits.

**Why it works:** Black's king is still in the middle and only his pawns are developed. In elite games
White scores about 65% after 6.Bc4, the best result of any line in this chapter. The quiet 6.Nxd4 is
possible but gives Black time for ...Bc5 and ...Nf6. }
6... Nf6 { [%cal Rf6e4] The most popular reply (about 40%). It develops and attacks the e4 pawn. }
7. O-O { [%cal Gf1f7] Castling instead of defending e4: every tempo goes into development, and the rook
joins the f-file behind the f7 target. } 7... Bc5 { [%cal Rc5g1,Rc5f2] Defends d4 and eyes g1, but leaves
f7 to the king alone. } 8. Ng5 { [%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] Doubles on f7. Black's natural move now loses. }
8... O-O? { [%cal Rg8f7] Castles into the combination. The king arrives on the f-file just in time for the
pin. }
( 8... Nxe4 { Grabs a pawn and leaves f7 to its fate. } 9. Bxf7+ { The study's picture of how fast Black's
position falls apart. } 9... Kd7? { The king is dragged up the board and cannot get back; the finish is
a mating attack. } 10. Nxe4 Bb6 11. Qg4+ Ke7 12. Qxg7 d3+ 13. Kh1 Kd7 14. Bc4+ Qe7 15. Qxe7+ Kxe7 16. Rf7+
Ke8 17. Nd6+ Kd8 18. Bg5# )
9. Nxf7! { [%cal Gg5f7] The knight sacrifice that makes the pin work. Black has to take it, and did so in
15 of 15 elite games. } 9... Rxf7 10. Bxf7+ { [%cal Gf1f6] The bishop sacrifice pulls the king onto f7. }
10... Kxf7 { [%csl Rf6,Rf7][%cal Gf1f6] **The point:** the king now stands on f7 with the rook on f1 behind
the f6 knight. The knight is pinned, so it cannot take anything and it cannot move.

Material is roughly level (two minor pieces for a rook and a pawn), but the pin decides the rest. }
11. Qh5+ { [%cal Gd1h5,Gh5c5] The queen checks on the diagonal that the pinned knight cannot cover, and
then takes the c5 bishop along the fifth rank. } ( 11. e5 { Also strong: the pawn attacks the pinned
knight. With the bishop on c5 the queen move wins more, but e5 is the way to win the knight when there
is nothing to capture on c5 (see the next line). } 11... Nd7 12. exf6 Nxf6 )
11... Kg8 { Kg8 or ...g6 makes no difference: the c5 bishop falls either way. } 12. Qxc5 { White is
the exchange up with a safe king; the black king is bare and the queenside undeveloped. } 12... Nxe4 13. Qc4+
Kh8 { **Verdict:** White wins material by force. Black's last resource is to take the e4 pawn, but the
knight and the d4 pawn are loose and White has the exchange. In elite games this sequence won 14 of 18,
and the study files it under the worst moves Black can make. } *

[Line "7...Be7 8.Ng5 O-O 9.Nxf7 and 11.e5"]
[Section "5...exd4: the gambit"]
[Summary "The same sacrifice against the quieter bishop move. With nothing on c5 to win, White wins the pinned knight with e5."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 5. Nf3 exd4 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 { [%cal Re7f6] The second most popular
seventh move (about a third of the games). The bishop covers f6 and the king's squares, so Black thinks
he is safe from Ng5. } 8. Ng5 { [%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] The same idea. Playing 8.e5 first is the alternative
and it scores poorly (2 wins in 11). } 8... O-O 9. Nxf7 Rxf7 10. Bxf7+ Kxf7
11. e5 { [%cal Ge5f6,Gf1f6] There is no bishop on c5 to win, but the f6 knight is pinned to the king and
the pawn attacks it. The knight cannot move without exposing the king to the rook, so it is lost. }
11... Kg8 { Gives the king a square and offers a pawn back. } 12. exf6 Bxf6 { **Verdict:** White has the
exchange for a pawn, a safe king and the d4 pawn to take back. In the 31 elite games with 11.e5 White won
20 and lost 9. Every one of the 40 players who reached this position castled on move 8, so the line has
not been tested against a Black who finds something else. } *

[Line "7...Be6 8.Bxe6 fxe6 9.e5: kicking the knight"]
[Section "5...exd4: the gambit"]
[Summary "Black challenges the bishop at once. After the exchange on e6 White kicks the knight with e5 and the Ng5 jump hits e6; the d4 pawn is regained with interest."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 5. Nf3 exd4 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. O-O Be6 { [%cal Re6c4] Challenges the bishop
before it can do any harm. Played in about one game in eight. } 8. Bxe6 { Gives up the bishop pair for a
pawn structure. } 8... fxe6 { Black's pawns are now e6, c6, d4: the e6 pawn is isolated once the e-file
opens, and the f-file is open for White's rook. } 9. e5 { [%cal Ge5f6] Attacks the knight. The e5 pawn
cramps Black and takes d5 and f6 from his pieces. } ( 9. Ng5 { Also possible: Ng5 hits e6 and h7. In
elite games it scored worse than e5 (one win in three). } 9... Bc5 )
9... Nd5 { [%cal Rd5f4,Rd5b4] The knight goes to d5, where it blocks the d-file and covers f4 and b4. }
( 9... Ne4 { Keeps the knight active on e4, and the elite results favour Black (8 wins for Black in 16). }
10. Nbd2 Nxd2 )
10. Ng5 { [%cal Gg5e6,Gd1d4] Attacks the e6 pawn and threatens to take on d4. Black has to answer both.
Elite games: 11 games, 10 wins for White. } 10... Qd7 { **Verdict:** a very small sample, but White has the
easier game: the d4 pawn will be regained (Qxd4), e6 is a weakness and f7 and the f-file belong to White.
The risk is the same as everywhere else in this chapter: the king on g1 faces the a7-g1 diagonal if a
dark-squared bishop reaches it. } *

[Line "6...Bg4 and 6...Bb4+: the early sixth moves"]
[Section "5...exd4: the gambit"]
[Summary "6...Bg4 walks into Bxf7+ and Ne5+; 6...Bb4+ forces White to give another pawn but gets a strong attack."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 5. Nf3 exd4 6. Bc4 Bg4 { [%cal Rg4f3] Pins the knight again and wants
to take on f3. }
( 6... Bb4+ { Black interposes the check. } 7. c3 { The move played in 19 of the 23 elite games. } 7... dxc3
8. Bxf7+ { [%cal Gc4f7] The elite choice (12 of 14 games). Black has taken two pawns, White has taken one
and dragged the king out. } 8... Ke7 9. Qb3 { **Verdict:** a real gambit. White is a pawn down and has
open lines against the king. Four elite games reached 9.Qb3, which is far too few to call it theory. It
is a sharp side branch; if you want to avoid it, recapture 6.Nxd4 against 5...exd4. } )
7. Bxf7+ { [%cal Gc4f7] The same tactic as in the 5...Bg4 line. } 7... Kxf7 8. Ne5+
{ [%cal Ge5f7,Ge5g4,Gd1g4] A fork of the king and the bishop on g4, which is also attacked by the queen. }
8... Ke8 9. Qxg4 { **Verdict:** White has regained the sacrificed pawn and taken away the black king's right
to castle; the elite results in this line (7 games) were 6 wins for White. } 9... Nf6 *
